    "title": "Montana Limits Nonresident Shed Hunting - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By KC ,\n9 hours ago Joe Raedle/Getty Images If you‚Äôve been chasing antlers these last few springs, you‚Äôve seen it. Out of state plates. Side by sides lined up at trailheads. Trucks parked where they should NOT be.\nMontana finally stepped in.\nA new law from Montana Fish, Wildlife, and Parks is now in effect that directly impacts nonresident shed hunters. The goal is simple. Reduce overcrowding and protect wildlife during a sensitive time of year.\nJoe Raedle/Getty Images Why The Law Was Passed Spring isn‚Äôt just treasure hunting season. It is survival season. Elk and deer are coming off a long winter. They are stressed. They are thin. They do not need people pushing them around hillsides so someone can grab a brown bone and flip it on eBay.\nThe pressure on Wildlife Management Areas has exploded in recent years. Residents have watched favorite spots turn into parking lots. That‚Äôs not how shed season used to feel. The new rule limits when nonresidents can collect antlers on certain public lands. Wildlife Management Areas, or WMAs, do not open for shed hunting until May 15th.\nThat date matters. It gives animals time to recover. It spreads out pressure. It puts Montana residents back on a more level playing field in their own backyard. Residents get first dibs on WMAs because the new law restricts non-residents from shed hunting until a week after the opening day.\nMiles Willis/Getty Images for Sotheby‚Äôs This Is About Balance Look, I am not anti-visitor. Tourism pays bills here. But when locals cannot enjoy their own public land without battling crowds during one of the shortest seasons of the year, something had to change.\nFWP made the call. And if you head out after May 15th, remember one more thing. It is spring in Montana. That means bears are up. Be bear aware. Carry spray. Make noise. Not everything needs to turn into a headline, especially a shed hunt.\nSee If You Can Identify These Montana Lakes Using Google Earth Images From above, Montana's lakes may be less recognizable than you might think. Test your knowledge--see if you recognize these Montana lakes from Google Earth images. Gallery Credit: Ashley Google/Canva Google #1 Google/Canva Canyon Ferry Lake Google/Canva Google #2 Google/Canva Flathead Lake Google/Canva Google #3 Getty Images/ThinkStock/Canva Saint Mary Lake Google/Canva Google #4 Townsquare Media/Canva Holland Lake Google/Canva Google #5 Townsquare Media/Canva Seeley Lake Google/Canva Google #6 Google/Canva Quake Lake 2",
    "summary": "A new law from Montana Fish, Wildlife, and Parks is now in effect that directly impacts nonresident shed hunters. Joe Raedle/Getty Images Why The Law Was Passed Spring isn‚Äôt just treasure hunting season. Residents get first dibs on WMAs because the new law restricts non-residents from shed hunting until a week after the opening day. But when locals cannot enjoy their own public land without battling crowds during one of the shortest seasons of the year, something had to change. Test your knowledge--see if you recognize these Montana lakes from Google Earth images.",
